<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">30 January 1649 · Whitehall, London</p><h1 class="sp-title">Charles I on the Scaffold</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Defeated in the English Civil War and tried for treason by a court established by Parliament, Charles I was led to a scaffold outside the Banqueting House. He spoke to those near him, maintaining to the end that a subject and a sovereign are entirely different things.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>I go from a corruptible to an incorruptible Crown, where no disturbance can be.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">King Charls his Speech made upon the Scaffold (1649).</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“I go from a corruptible to an incorruptible Crown, where no disturbance can be.” — Charles I, Charles I on the Scaffold" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-charles-i-on-the-scaffold.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>The execution of an anointed king shocked Europe, and England became a republic. Eleven years later the monarchy was restored under his son, Charles II, and the men who had signed the death warrant were hunted down.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/state/commonwealth-of-england">Commonwealth of England</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuart-restoration">Stuart Restoration</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-england">Charles II</a></div></div></section>
